To automate the process of managing model versions in dbt, you can implement a custom macro and a post-hook. The macro, named `create_latest_version_view`, is designed to run only if the model is versioned and is the latest version. This means that if you have multiple versions of a model, the macro will check the versioning information and only execute if the current model is the most recent one. If it is, the macro will create a new relation that incorporates the model's name, effectively creating a view that points to the latest version. This automation reduces manual effort and ensures that the latest version is always accessible without additional configuration from the user. For example, if you have a model that is frequently updated, this macro will help maintain a seamless experience for users querying the data, as they will always be directed to the most current version without needing to change their queries. Additionally, to implement this macro, you would define it in your dbt project as follows: 

```sql
{% macro create_latest_version_view(model_name) %}
  {% if is_versioned(model_name) and is_latest_version(model_name) %}
    create view {{ model_name }}_latest as
    select * from {{ model_name }};
  {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
```

You would then call this macro in your model's post-hook like this:

```yaml
post-hook:
  - '{{ create_latest_version_view(this.name) }}'
```

It's important to consider potential edge cases, such as what happens if a model is not versioned or if there are no updates to the model. In these cases, the macro should handle these scenarios gracefully, perhaps by logging a message or skipping the creation of the view.
